//@ unimplemented "Every import form in the file parses, `import * from m;` included, and this note's claim that none of them resolves is corrected here, 2026-08-27: measured from the repository root, which is where the corpus is compiled, all seven resolve and the only diagnostic in the file is `Undefined macro 'macros.magic_add!'` at 23:20. Line 4's `import { printf } from stdio;` is worth naming separately, because it is the measurement that disproved interfaces.fin's note -- printf is findable through the bundled library today, so a sample that fails on printf fails for want of a binding and not for want of a search path. The working-directory dependence this note recorded is real and still there, and I re-measured it rather than deleting it: run from a different directory, line 3's `import { Vector3 } from \"tests/samples/structs.fin\"` gives `module not found: tests/samples/structs.fin` and takes line 7's `import \"tests/samples/macros.fin\"` down with it. A quoted path is tried relative to the importing file first, then as an absolute path, then through the search paths -- and the search-path set ends in the working directory, which is the step that resolves these two from the repository root and nothing else. So the file's own claim on line 9, that the compiler needs `FIN_LIBS` or `--fin-libs` to know where a pathless library lives, is answered: `import \"somelib\"` and `import * from somelib` on 9 and 11 reach `lib/std/somelib`, and 14's `import { HashMap } from hashmap::std` reaches `lib/std/hashmap.fin`. `macros.magic_add!` is booked and not repaired, and the sample is wrong about itself in a way no repair reaches. Line 7's comment says the import brings macros in \"as `macros` by default so it can be used like `macros.magic_add!(...)`\", but tests/samples/macros.fin declares no such macro -- it is five lines, an `@define printf` and the comment \"Truncated (Rust-Like upcoming...)\" -- and `magic_add` appears in no other file in the tree, so the qualified call has nothing to resolve to. Every repair available is destructive or invented: deleting line 23 removes the construct the file exists to specify, and declaring `magic_add!` writes a macro the corpus does not prove. The nearest thing to a witness is tests/samples/macro_definitions.fin, which is the corpus's only macro-definition text and is entirely inside a `/* [WIP] */` block, so it declares nothing either. This correction replaced the note line in place and changed no other line, so the file is 24 lines as it was"
import { Vector3 } from "tests/samples/structs.fin"; // This is allowed and ONLY imports the symbol (Vector3) [supports special functions, macros, ...]
import { printf } from stdio; // this looks in builtins directory which we haven't implemented yet but it looks for either a file named "stdio.fin" or "stdio.finlib" OR looks for a directory named "stdio" and looks for a file named "printf.fin" in it (THERE ARE SO MANY POSSIBILIES THAT CAN HAPPEN WITH DOTS TOO like "stdio.buffers" and more)
import networking; // looks for this in builtins and imports the whole module which allows to be used like "networking.invoke()"
import networking as net; // this is using the "as" keyword for name changing which is super useful
import "tests/samples/macros.fin"; // this is a normal file import which imports macros as "macros" by default so it can be used like "macros.magic_add!(...)"

import "somelib"; // IMPORTANT: THIS IS SO IMPORTANT BECAUSE WE DO NOT GIVE ANY PATH TO IT SO HOW DOES THE COMPILER KNOW WHERE TO IMPORT THIS LIBRARY, as we assume somelib is a directory containing modules. The compiler also checks an environment variable named "FIN_LIBS" which has multiple paths of imports (specially builtins) and uses those to search for libraries (this is how our package manager "finn" can easily tell our compiler which environment to use using "--fin-libs" flag)

import * from somelib; // this will import ALL symbols from the somelib library

//since namespaces exists we also allow a way to import stuff from namespaces explicitly:
import { HashMap } from hashmap::std; // this means there is a namespace in hashmap library and we explicitly import the `HashMap` symbol from that namespace using COLONCOLON



fun main() <noret> {
    let x <auto> = Vector3{x: 10, y: 10, z: 10};

    printf("Hello i am imported from stdio");

    let y <auto> = macros.magic_add!(10, 20);
  }
